

ANSICAT(1)           UNIX Programmer's Manual          ANSICAT(1)



NAME
     ansicat - show ANSI art on a terminal

SYNOPSIS
     aannssiiccaatt [ --bb _b_a_u_d ] [ _f_i_l_e ... ]

DESCRIPTION
     _A_n_s_i_c_a_t copies each _f_i_l_e, or the standard input, to
     the standard output byte for byte, for a terminal that
     shows ANSI art: code page 437 text and ECMA-48 control
     sequences written for a PC's ANSI.SYS.  The Second Sight
     card's console, /dev/ttyv3, shows sixteen colours on six-
     teen backgrounds and the code page's glyphs; the IIgs
     screen shows the art in its four colours and ASCII.

     Before the first file it resets the attributes, clears the
     screen and homes the cursor.  Each file is copied up to its
     first ^Z, the DOS end-of-file byte that precedes a SAUCE
     record, so the record is not shown.  The terminal's LLITOUT
     mode is set for the copy, since without it the tty strips
     the eighth bit of every byte; the local mode word and the
     attributes are put back on exit and on an interrupt.

     --bb _b_a_u_d
          Pace the output at _b_a_u_d/10 bytes a second, as the
          file arrived over a modem; an ANSI animation is drawn
          for that timing.  10 to 115200.

     Each console keeps its own default colours: ESC [ 8 ]
     makes the current colours the default, as on the Linux
     console, and ESC [ 0 m returns to them.

SEE ALSO
     color(1), stty(1)

BUGS
     Blinking text shows as a bright background (iCE colour);
     a SAUCE record's flags are not read.  Bytes $01-$1F are
     controls, so their code page glyphs are not shown.  An
     ESC [ 2 J inside a file does not home the cursor.  Art
     taller than the screen scrolls.



